  the open set that must surround 1 is a progression 1 + (p₁p₂⋯pₖ)ℤ. Its members are exactly the
  numbers that leave remainder 1 on division by the product of all the primes. That is Euclid's N in
  different clothing.

  [1mWhat the three share[0m

  All three proofs rest on the same fact: every integer above 1 has a prime factor. They differ in
  what they build around it. Euclid builds a single number. Euler builds an identity that carries
  the entire multiplicative structure of the integers. Furstenberg builds a space in which a
  finiteness assumption collides with the size of open sets.

  They also differ in what they give back. Euclid's proof gives a method for finding a new prime.
  Euler's gives a measure of how many primes there are. Furstenberg's gives no new information about
  primes at all, but it shows that the language of topology can express a purely arithmetic fact.
  Proofs of this theorem are still being published, largely for that reason.

